//! Entries slice — Batch 196 (Plan 195) — the `TensorFold` staged pack's
//! A− leads (2): the affine stored-block address that lets the pipeliner
//! fly (L: layout-for-addressability — the staircase
//! `(b // 4) * 32 * SBN + ((b % 4) * 16) // 2 * SBN` is simply
//! `b * (8 * SBN)`, and only the affine spelling gets the weight traffic
//! in flight), and the register-width top-k fallback to a tiled radix
//! select with THE SAME LISTS past the `SELECT_REGS` spill cliff
//! (D: register-budget-keyed dispatch). Plus one instance rider on B176
//! `dtype-native-source-read-with-shared-admission-predicate` (the
//! `lane_fuse` g32 admission instance) — the rider text lives on the host
//! entry in `entries_gpu_51.rs`, not here.
//!
//! Source: `ashhart/tensorfold` @ `c4646171139ee8a3c38103eaa1699dad226ec12b`
//! (`TensorFold` v0.6.0 tag; Apache-2.0 — license flipped MIT→Apache-2.0 at
//! 0.6.0; read at the pin — every load-bearing quote verified VERBATIM at
//! the pin before landing).
//!
//! Honesty flags: both rules are MECHANISM + DISPATCH — the spill-cliff
//! constant, the dispatch predicate and the affine address are in-source;
//! every timing / state-identity figure (the 26-27 ms vs 0.8 ms spill, the
//! 26.4→0.7 / 27.2→1.5 ms recovery table, the 21% decode-cost share) is
//! COMMIT-MESSAGE-ONLY (a2dba9c / 443ad4a) and labeled as such in-rule.
//! Both `NotBenchable` here (no Triton validator in this corpus) — the
//! lane offers ride the rule texts.

use super::KernelRule;

#[allow(clippy::too_many_lines)]
pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── L: the stored-block address the advancing loop computes is a
        // STAIRCASE in the step index when derived from the K-block/row
        // decomposition — the layout fact (8 tile rows a stored macro
        // block) gives the linear spelling, and only the affine address
        // lets the hardware pipeliner put the weight traffic in flight.
        // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "affine-block-address-lets-pipeliner-fly".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (affine block address lets pipeliner fly): a weight table stored in MACRO BLOCKS offers two spellings of the same advancing-loop address — the layout-obvious one and the linear one — and only the affine one gets the weight traffic in flight. TensorFold's nvfp4 grouped matmul first addressed the packed blocks the layout was written in — `(b // 4) * 32 * SBN + ((b % 4) * 16) // 2 * SBN` — which is a STAIRCASE in the loop step b: 'The address `(b // 4) * 32 * SBN + ((b % 4) * 16) // 2 * SBN` is a staircase in the step (a stored macro block holds 8 rows of the tile), and a load whose address the pipeliner cannot see as affine is not put in flight; it is the same address as `b * 8 * SBN` (16 * SBN for the pattern tables, whose macro block holds 16 rows)' (443ad4a, at-sha). The in-source form at the pin names the win from the load's side: '# 8 bytes a block (low nibble: even input), 8 * SBN apart: an address the pipeliner follows' (nvfp4.py:312), and the fixed load is `tl.load(tile + b * (8 * SBN) + (r16 // 2)[:, None] * SBN + local[None, :])` (:313): the tile base advances AFFINELY with b (one stride per step); only the within-block row/column terms stay data-dependent. DECISION CONTENT — LAYOUT FOR ADDRESSABILITY: when a packed layout makes the advancing address a staircase (a div/mod remix of the step index every iteration), re-derive the address from what the layout FACT gives (8 tile rows per stored macro block — 'packed: 32 bytes a K block, 8 rows a block', nvfp4.py:301 — so block b's base is b * 8 * SBN, linear in b) so the HARDWARE PIPELINER can see the coming addresses and overlap the weight traffic — the priority datum: 'the weight traffic is what the step actually waits on' (443ad4a, commit-message-only). The staircase spelling was an accident of deriving the address from the K-block/row decomposition instead of from the block count; the identity is exact (b = 4q + r gives 32q·SBN + 8r·SBN = 8·SBN·b), so the rewrite is output-identical by arithmetic, not by tolerance. REGIME: any packed/staged weight table whose advancing-loop address is rebuilt per step from a div/mod decomposition of the step index, where the rows-per-stored-block fact makes the base linear. ANTI-REGIME: addresses that genuinely differ per K-block (no rows-per-block identity — there the staircase may be the honest form); loop-INVARIANT addresses (that is hoist-bit-address's axis, not this one); a layout whose macro block holds a non-multiple of the consumed tile rows. NUMBERS HONESTY: the decode-cost datum ('the E2M1 and E4M3 decodes together are 21% of a grouped step at the served geometry (7 rows x 11 slots, 72 distinct experts)', dev/deqcost.py) is commit-message-only — the harness is not in the tree at the pin; the affine-address win itself ships mechanism-only (no per-address timing). NotBenchable here (no Triton validator in the corpus). KINSHIP (do not double-count): hoist-bit-address-out-of-packed-row-sweep (B) hoists a loop-INVARIANT bit address OUT of its row loop (pos_in_tile never varies) — THIS makes an ADVANCING address AFFINE in the step index so the pipeliner overlaps it (invariant-hoist vs advancing-affine: distinct axes); strength-reduce-div/mod-pow2 (O) rewrites the staircase's div/mod ARITHMETIC in place and leaves the address a staircase (two cheaper terms, still non-affine) — this REPLACES the address expression via the layout identity; strided-view-offset-fit32-launch-proof (Batch 195, O) proves an address FITS 32 bits before dispatch — a different question entirely. CITE: TensorFold v0.6.0 @ c4646171, Apache-2.0 (license flipped MIT→Apache-2.0 at 0.6.0) — src/tensorfold/families/qwen4_exp/cuda/nvfp4.py:301,312-313 + commit 443ad4a (perf(nvfp4): one reduce launch for the fp32 faces, and cheaper blocks in _fp4mm). Batch 196 / Plan 195.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the packed-block address is derived from the K-block/row\n// decomposition of the step index — a STAIRCASE in the loop step b.\n// The hardware pipeliner cannot see (b/4)*32*SBN + ((b%4)*16)/2*SBN\n// as affine in b, so the next iterations' weight loads are not put in\n// flight: the step waits on the weight traffic (the decodes together\n// measured 21% of a grouped step — commit-message-only, 443ad4a).\nconst SBN: usize = 512; // stride between rows of the table, words\n\nfn block_addr_staircase(b: usize) -> usize {\n    (b / 4) * 32 * SBN + ((b % 4) * 16) / 2 * SBN // a staircase in b\n}\n\nfn grouped_step_weights(steps: usize) -> Vec<usize> {\n    (0..steps).map(block_addr_staircase).collect() // non-affine advances\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the LAYOUT FACT gives the linear spelling — a stored\n// macro block holds 8 tile rows (\"8 bytes a block (low nibble: even\n// input), 8 * SBN apart: an address the pipeliner follows\"), so block\n// b's base is b * (8 * SBN): affine in the loop step, and the\n// pipeliner puts the weight traffic in flight. (16 * SBN for the\n// pattern tables, whose macro block holds 16 rows.)\nconst SBN: usize = 512;\nconst ROWS_PER_STORED_BLOCK: usize = 8;\n\nfn block_addr_affine(b: usize) -> usize {\n    b * (ROWS_PER_STORED_BLOCK * SBN) // = the staircase, linearly\n}\n\nfn grouped_step_weights(steps: usize) -> Vec<usize> {\n    (0..steps).map(block_addr_affine).collect() // affine: overlaps\n}\n\n// the identity is EXACT (b = 4q + r: 32q*SBN + 8r*SBN = 8*SBN*b), so\n// the rewrite is output-identical by arithmetic — not by tolerance.\nfn staircase_equivalence(b: usize) -> bool {\n    block_addr_affine(b) == (b / 4) * 32 * SBN + ((b % 4) * 16) / 2 * SBN\n}".to_string(),
        },
        // ── D: a per-row top-k selection kernel that holds the row's FULL
        // score set in registers has a SPILL cliff; dispatch past it to a
        // tiled radix select that produces THE SAME LISTS (cut by radix
        // select one byte a pass from the top, ties by lowest block id,
        // in block order) — output-identical, so the cliff crossing is
        // invisible to the consumer. ─────────────────────────────────────
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "register-width-topk-falls-back-to-tiled-radix-select".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (register-width topk falls back to tiled radix select): a per-row top-k selection kernel that holds a row's FULL score set in registers has a SPILL CLIFF, and the fix is register-budget-keyed dispatch with an OUTPUT-IDENTICAL fallback — never a compromise selection. The cliff is pinned in-source: 'SELECT_REGS = 32768  # _select holds a row's block scores in registers up to this many; past it they spill' (attention.py:13), and the launcher dispatches on the row's next-pow2 width — 'if width <= SELECT_REGS:' takes _select, else _select_tiles (attention.py:357); the contract rides the launcher docstring: '_select while ``blocks`` fit its registers, ``_select_tiles`` (the same lists) past them' (:353 — the staged row cited :353 for the dispatch; the dispatch itself sits at :357, line drift disclosed). The spill side is not a graceful slowdown: 'Up to 32,768 blocks (131,072 keys) that fits; past it the row spills to local memory, and one layer's 256-row prompt block took 26-27 ms instead of 0.8 ms on a DGX Spark, from one position to the next. A 256k-token prompt pays that in 512 row blocks x 12 layers: about 165 s, half of its prefill' (a2dba9c, commit-message-only). The fallback keeps the OUTPUT CONTRACT, not just approximate top-k: '_select_tiles lists the same blocks for those rows. It reads the row in tiles, finds the cut (the 512th largest order-preserving key) by radix select, one byte a pass from the top, then places every key above the cut and the lowest-id keys equal to it, in block order, as _select does' — THE SAME LISTS (ties by lowest block id, block order), which is what makes the dispatch legal at any width. Exactness evidence (commit-message-only): 'the state after 140k- and 250k-token prompts (first token, streams, DeltaNet states and conv windows, every KV, indexer and pooled cache, MTP caches) is identical with both selections, with bf16 and int8 KV, and so are 64 drafted tokens after 140k, greedy and sampled. The teacher-forced corpus gives 119,980 of 119,980 values equal to the stored reference.' Measured recovery (commit-message-only, DGX Spark per layer): a 256-row prompt block 26.4 → 0.7 ms at 135k keys and 27.2 → 1.5 ms at 262k; a 7-row decode window 0.51 → 0.25 ms at 262k. DECISION CONTENT — a register-budget cliff is a DISPATCH boundary, not a tuning knob: pick the kernel per row width at launch (budget-keyed), and make the past-cliff kernel uphold the byte-output contract (same cut rule, same tie order) so crossing the cliff is invisible to the consumer. REGIME: per-row selection/scoring kernels whose working set is the row width; any next_pow2(width) budget cliff. ANTI-REGIME: rows that always fit (the dispatch is dead — keep the single kernel); a fallback that only approximates top-k (wrong lists — the output contract is what licenses the dispatch); an UNMEASURED cliff constant (SELECT_REGS is the source's own pinned number — re-derive per arch and per compiler, never borrow it). NUMBERS HONESTY: the cliff constant + the dispatch are in-source; every timing / state-identity figure is commit-message-only (a2dba9c). NotBenchable here (no Triton validator in the corpus). KINSHIP (do not double-count): wide-verify-warp-specialized-scores-in-registers-pv (Batch 178, E) CHOOSES the registers posture by query width WITHIN the fit regime (scores stay in registers as the PV operand) — this handles the SPILL side (past the budget) with an identical-output fallback kernel; compose, never merge (one picks the in-register shape, one leaves the register regime entirely); warp-bitonic-topk-merge-shuffle (B) owns the in-register top-k MERGE mechanism this rule's _select arm is an instance of. CITE: TensorFold v0.6.0 @ c4646171, Apache-2.0 (license flipped MIT→Apache-2.0 at 0.6.0) — src/tensorfold/families/qwen4_exp/cuda/attention.py:13,353,357 + commit a2dba9c (perf: Flash Next lists QSA blocks past 131,072 keys in tiles, same lists). Batch 196 / Plan 195. RIDER (Batch 213 / Plan 213, ggml-org/llama.cpp @ 71ad0590f4808b6202f9213d166913858c73b1bc #28713 -- the GPU SHAPE-KEYED regime face): this rule owns the register-budget cliff with an output-identical fallback; the upstream TOP_K dispatcher adds the second dispatch axis -- ALGORITHM selection by (nrows, ncols, SM count) with a fixed-launch-cost amortization term. The decision boundary verbatim (commit message): 'bitonic for short rows, radix select for several long rows, and DeviceTopK or CUB argsort for a single long row' (the #28547/#29278 boundary); the wave-of-blocks admission verbatim: 'bitonic stays in use for rows up to a padded 1024 while the rows fit in one wave of blocks (nrows <= number of SMs)' -- in code the bitonic_few_rows predicate 'nrows > GGML_CUDA_TOP_K_NROWS_THRESHOLD && ncols <= GGML_CUDA_TOP_K_NCOLS_THRESHOLD_BITONIC_FEW_ROWS && nrows <= ...nsm' (top-k.cu:304-307; the padded row's shared-memory fit is checked at dispatch). The AMORTIZATION law verbatim: 'radix select pays a fixed cost of about a dozen launches that only amortizes over more rows'. The SCRATCH bound verbatim: 'Radix select now processes rows in chunks so its scratch memory stays bounded, and the bitonic path keeps its chunking' -- ggml_cuda_chunk_nrows with 'const int chunk_bytes = 1 << 26;' (64 MB) and the call-site comment 'chunk the rows to bound the histogram memory to 64 MB'. The int64 axis: 'do the TOP_K radix blocks_per_row math in int64_t' + supports_op gates BOTH TOP_K and ARGSORT at 'op->src[0]->ne[0] <= INT_MAX' (the old no-CUB 'ne[0] <= 1024' TOP_K gate removed). Helper sharing both axes, verbatim bullets: 'share the row chunking helper between argsort and top-k' (argsort_f32_i32_cuda_cub_chunk_nrows renamed ggml_cuda_chunk_nrows, hoisted out of the CUB ifdef, declared in argsort.cuh) and 'share one sort helper between the bitonic and CUB TOP_K paths' (top_k_argsort_cuda with a use_cub flag). Measured, source-reported (commit message): 'On qwen4exp at 34,816 tokens this cuts top-k from 1,671,253 launches / 5,761.8 ms to 2,329 / 941.8 ms' -- a ~717x launch reduction at ~6.1x wall. The two dispatch axes COMPOSE, they do not compete: this rule budgets REGISTERS within a shape's regime; #28713 picks the ALGORITHM across regimes where launch-count economics and the scratch bound decide -- a short-row/one-wave shape staying bitonic is the same output-identity discipline at the shape boundary. Thresholds stay build-time overridable and HIP/MUSA keep their own constants (1024 ncols caps) -- the regime table is per-backend measured, never borrowed. NotBenchable here (no CUDA validator in the corpus; the qwen4exp figures are source-reported). Verified verbatim at the pin (the standing clone fetched from ggml-org upstream). Batch 213 rider.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: ONE selection kernel for every row width — the row's full\n// score set (next_pow2(blocks) values) is held in registers, and past\n// the register file it SPILLS to local memory with no fallback: one\n// layer's 256-row prompt block took 26-27 ms instead of 0.8 ms,\n// position to position (commit-message-only, a2dba9c).\nconst SELECT_REGS: usize = 32_768; // the cliff exists but is never read\n\nfn launch_select_naive(scores: &[f32], ids: &mut Vec<usize>, top: usize) {\n    let _width = scores.len().next_power_of_two(); // may exceed SELECT_REGS\n    select_in_registers(scores, ids, top); // spills past the cliff\n}\n\nfn select_in_registers(scores: &[f32], ids: &mut Vec<usize>, top: usize) {\n    let mut order: Vec<usize> = (0..scores.len()).collect();\n    order.sort_by(|a, b| scores[*b].total_cmp(&scores[*a]));\n    ids.clear();\n    ids.extend(order.into_iter().take(top));\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: budget-keyed dispatch at launch — `_select` while the\n// row's next-pow2 width fits the registers, `_select_tiles` (THE SAME\n// LISTS) past them. The tiled arm reads the row in tiles, finds the\n// cut (the k-th largest order-preserving key) by radix select — one\n// byte a pass from the top — then places every key above the cut and\n// the lowest-id keys equal to it, IN BLOCK ORDER, as the register arm\n// does: identical output at any width.\nconst SELECT_REGS: usize = 32_768;\n\nfn launch_select(scores: &[f32], ids: &mut Vec<usize>, top: usize) {\n    let width = scores.len().next_power_of_two();\n    if width <= SELECT_REGS {\n        select_in_registers(scores, ids, top); // the register kernel\n    } else {\n        select_tiled_radix(scores, ids, top); // the same lists, past the cliff\n    }\n}\n\nfn select_in_registers(scores: &[f32], ids: &mut Vec<usize>, top: usize) {\n    let mut order: Vec<usize> = (0..scores.len()).collect();\n    order.sort_by(|a, b| scores[*b].total_cmp(&scores[*a]));\n    ids.clear();\n    ids.extend(order.into_iter().take(top));\n}\n\n// the tiled fallback: cut = the k-th largest order-preserving key by\n// radix select (one byte a pass from the top), then every key ABOVE\n// the cut + the lowest-id keys EQUAL to it, in block order — ties by\n// lowest block id, exactly the register arm's order.\nfn select_tiled_radix(scores: &[f32], ids: &mut Vec<usize>, top: usize) {\n    let cut = nth_largest(scores, top);\n    let mut picks: Vec<usize> = (0..scores.len()).filter(|&b| scores[b] > cut).collect();\n    for b in 0..scores.len() {\n        if picks.len() == top {\n            break;\n        }\n        if scores[b] == cut {\n            picks.push(b); // ties: the lowest block ids, in block order\n        }\n    }\n    ids.clear();\n    ids.extend(picks);\n}\n\nfn nth_largest(scores: &[f32], k: usize) -> f32 {\n    let mut buf: Vec<f32> = scores.to_vec();\n    buf.sort_by(|a, b| b.total_cmp(a));\n    buf[k - 1]\n}".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod batch196_slice_tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn two_rules_all_unique_ids() {
        let rules = entries();
        assert_eq!(rules.len(), 2);
        let mut ids: Vec<&str> = rules.iter().map(|r| r.rule_id.as_str()).collect();
        let n = ids.len();
        ids.sort_unstable();
        ids.dedup();
        assert_eq!(ids.len(), n, "rule_ids must be unique");
    }
}
